## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order_captured.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="5 0 1\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled index first, then value) — the index's
//! VALUE, not only its side effects, is taken before the right-hand
//! side. In `ys[i] = bump(mut i)` the index reads `i` (0), then
//! `bump` sets `i` to 1 and returns 5, and the store lands at index
//! 0: `5 0 1`. Value first would store at index 1 (`0 5 1`), which is
//! what the checked lane answered before s183.
## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order_compound.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="idx\nval\n8\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled index first, then value) — a compound
//! store: `xs[idx()] += val()` evaluates the index, then the
//! right-hand side, then reads, combines and writes the element.
//! Before s183 the checked lane ran `val` first.
## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="idx\nval\n7\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled 2026-09-26: index first, then value) —
//! s182's control, verbatim. In `xs[idx()] = val()` the place's index
//! operand is evaluated before the right-hand side, left to right as
//! `[mem.model.order]` reads; the element's address is minted after
//! the right-hand side (`[mem.model.place.rhs]`). `idx val 7` on the
//! checked, native and release lanes and on lupin. Before s183 the
//! checked lane printed `val idx 7` (trunk a201c518 and every release
//! through 0.2.17); native, release and lupin already printed this.
## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order_map.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="key\nval\n7 1\nkey\ngrow\n7 65\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order, type.map
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled 2026-09-26: index first, then value) —
//! the `Map` twin of `ctl_store_order.lu`. `m[key()] = val()`
//! evaluates the key before the right-hand side (`key val`). The
//! second store is both sentences of `[mem.model.place.rhs]` at once:
//! `m[key()] = grow(mut m)` evaluates the key, then the right-hand
//! side (which inserts 64 keys and rehashes the map), and only then
//! inserts — into the grown map: `7 65`. Before s183 the checked lane
//! ran the right-hand side first in both stores (`val key`, `grow
//! key`); native, release and lupin 0.1.40 already printed this.
## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order_nested_index.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="i\nj\nval\n7\na\nb\nc\nval\n9\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled 2026-09-26: index first, then value) —
//! a place with several index operands evaluates them left to right,
//! outermost first, each exactly once, and then the right-hand side:
//! `g[i][j] = val()` prints `i j val`, `h[a][b][c] = val()` prints
//! `a b c val`. Before s183 the checked lane printed `val` first;
//! native and release already printed this. lupin 0.1.40 evaluates
//! every operand but the last TWICE in a store (`i i j val`, `a b a b
//! c val`; reads are once) — wolf-interp#145.
## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order_nested.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="c\nval\n7\np\nval\n7\nx\ngrow\n7 65\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled 2026-09-26: index first, then value) —
//! the index under a longer place. `cs[at("c", 0)].n = val()` runs
//! the index before the right-hand side (`c val`), and so does a store
//! through a `mut` parameter (`p val`). The last store is both
//! sentences of `[mem.model.place.rhs]` at once: `xs[at("x", 0)] =
//! grow(mut xs)` runs the index (`x`), then the right-hand side
//! (`grow`, which pushes 64 elements and moves the buffer), and only
//! then locates the element — so the store lands at index 0 of the
//! grown list: `7 65`. Before s183 the checked lane ran every
//! right-hand side first; native, release and lupin 0.1.40 already
//! printed this. Two index levels are `ctl_store_order_nested_index.lu`.
## corpus/memory/ctl_store_order_raw.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="idx\nval\n9\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.rhs, mem.model.order, mem.unsafe.scope
//!
//! s183 (wolf-lang#452, ruled 2026-09-26: index first, then value) —
//! a raw-pointer element store orders its operands the same way:
//! `p[idx()] = val()` evaluates the pointer, then the index, then the
//! right-hand side, then writes. Before s183 the checked lane ran
//! `val` first; native, release and lupin 0.1.40 already printed this.
