## corpus/memory/elem_move_one_place.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="1 2\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.elem, mem.tier0.move.2, mem.model.place
//!
//! s182 (wolf-lang#446) — `move xs[0]` on a `List[int]` and then a read
//! of `xs[1]`. Through 0.2.17 every element of a container was ONE
//! place on the compiler (`Proj::Opaque`), so the move of `xs[0]`
//! emptied `xs[_]` and the read of `xs[1]` was E1001 on both wolfgang
//! lanes while lupin, which models elements one by one, ran it: the
//! documented conservatism row this file pinned.
//!
//! eg01 (EGC's EG1) closes it: `[mem.model.place.elem]` item 1(a) — two
//! integer-literal indices whose values differ are distinct places, so
//! the move leaves `xs[1]` readable. `1 2` on checked, native, release
//! and lupin 0.1.40. The driver test `element_move_conservatism_lanes.rs`
//! asserts every lane; `elem_move_same_const_read.lu` keeps the moved
//! element itself unreadable.
## corpus/memory/elem_pool_handle_revive.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="2\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.elem, mem.tier0.move.4
//!
//! eg01b — R3 through a `Pool` handle local: a handle is a `Copy`
//! value, so `move p[h]` … `p[h] = take t` with `h` unwritten between
//! revives the element. Two handles stay one place for every other rule
//! (item 2; `elem_pool_handles_one_place.lu`). 0.2.17 accepted this
//! through any-store revival (wolf-lang#460); eg01 refused it (E1001).
//! `2` on checked, native and release; lupin declines `Pool` by name.
## corpus/memory/elem_same_index_revive.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="1 3\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.elem, mem.tier0.move.4, mem.region.edge.elem
//!
//! eg00 — `[mem.model.place.elem]` item 4's R3: take an element out
//! through a run-time index, work on it, store it back through the
//! SAME index (`i` unassigned between). The store provably denotes the
//! moved element, so it revives it and the read is legal. `1 3` on
//! checked, native, release and lupin 0.1.40.
//!
//! Today this compiles for a worse reason: any index store revives the
//! collapsed element place (wolf-lang#460). When that revival narrows
//! to item 3's must-rule, this row is what must keep compiling — R3 is
//! the rule that keeps it.
## corpus/memory/elem_str_key_revive.lu
//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="3 7 2\n")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: mem.model.place.elem, mem.tier0.move.4
//!
//! eg01b — `[mem.model.place.elem]` item 4's R3 over a `Copy` local
//! that is not an integer (the maintainer's ruling, 2026-09-26): read a
//! value out of a `Map[str, List[int]]` through a `str` key, work on it,
//! store it back through the SAME key. `k` is unwritten between, so the
//! store provably denotes the element the read emptied and revives it.
//! Both spellings: a key parameter (`bump`) and a key local (`main`).
//!
//! 0.2.17 accepted this through any-store revival (wolf-lang#460);
//! eg01 narrowed revival to R3 over integer locals and refused it
//! (E1001 at `main`'s read after the store). `3 7 2` on checked,
//! native, release and lupin 0.1.40.
